@inject Microsoft.Extensions.Options.IOptions<LearningHub.Nhs.WebUI.Configuration.Settings> options
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Use of artificial intelligence in digital learning resources";
}
@section styles {
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="~/css/nhsuk/pages/Policies.css" asp-append-version="true" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="~/css/nhsuk/pages/staticpages.css" asp-append-version="true" />
}
<div class="nhsuk-width-container app-width-container">
    <div class="nhsuk-main-wrapper ">
        <h1 class="nhsuk-heading-xl"> Use of artificial intelligence in digital learning resources</h1>
        <article class="nhsuk-u-reading-width nhsuk-u-font-size-19">
            <section>
                <h1 class="nhsuk-heading-xl">Use of artificial intelligence in digital learning resources</h1>

                <p>This statement covers the use of artificial intelligence (AI) to create high-quality, accessible digital learning resources, including e-learning, webinars, videos and animations, for all health and care professions by the NHS England (NHSE) Technology Enhanced Learning (TEL) Content Development Team.</p>

                <p>This statement was prepared by the Task and Finish Group on behalf of the Content Development Team and approved by the AI and Healthcare Education Steering Group on 26 March 2026. It reflects the general principles we adhere to regarding the use of AI in the creation of digital learning resources.</p>

                <p>The statement will be reviewed regularly to consider emerging tools and the development of our full governance process and policy, or every year, whichever is sooner.</p>

                <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l">Statement of principles</h2>

                <p>The NHSE TEL Content Development Team is committed to producing high-quality learning resources that improve patient care and deliver value for taxpayers’ money. We prioritise accuracy, reliability and trust in all our educational content, while continuously improving the quality, efficiency and speed of development. This ensures the NHS workforce has access to up-to-date learning resources that support professional development and enable the delivery of excellent care.</p>

                <p>AI refers to the use of digital technology to create systems capable of performing tasks commonly thought to require human intelligence. For more information on AI and its applications in the NHS, please refer to the <a href="https://transform.england.nhs.uk/ai-lab/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NHS AI Lab</a> and the <a href="https://transform.england.nhs.uk/ai-lab/explore-all-resources/understand-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NHSE AI Guide</a>.</p>

                <p>The TEL team is committed to using AI in a transparent, accessible and ethical way across our digital learning platforms.</p>

                <p>Learning content may include text and media, such as video, audio, graphics, photographs and metadata, that have been generated or enhanced using AI. AI tools and AI-assisted processes may also have been used during the research, planning, discovery, design and evaluation stages of development.</p>

                <p>All AI-generated content is reviewed and approved by subject matter experts (SMEs) and content leads to ensure accuracy, relevance and appropriateness. Where applicable, AI-generated images, audio and video may be labelled to maintain transparency and trust. All AI tools used in content development are subject to NHS England’s governance protocols, including ethical review, bias mitigation and data protection compliance. In addition to human review, we are investigating the effectiveness of systems and processes that standardise and document our efforts to reduce bias and remove harmful stereotypes.</p>

                <p>We are actively working to ensure that AI-enhanced content meets accessibility standards, including the use of alternative text (alt text) and descriptive attributes for screen readers.</p>

                <p>NHS England does not use AI to replace human judgement or expertise. Instead, AI is used to support and accelerate content creation while maintaining high standards of quality and inclusivity.</p>

                <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l">General principles of use</h2>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Educational impact</h3>

                <p>Our use of AI must enhance learning outcomes and support the delivery of safe, effective care. Its use should be evaluated against learning objectives and the learner experience. Our approach ensures that AI is a targeted, evidence-based tool in the educational environment, directly contributing to better learning outcomes and a smoother transition to competent clinical practice. Its value is continuously evaluated against our core mission of safe and effective healthcare education, with input from our SMEs.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Leadership in practice</h3>

                <p>As a national team, we recognise our responsibility to lead and support the wider learning experience development community. We acknowledge that SMEs, learners and educators are already using AI to create and interact with learning content. We aim to adapt our approach to maximise the benefits and minimise the risks of AI adoption when developing content and learning from its use.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">AI, digital and data literacy</h3>

                <p>We support the development of AI literacy across our team and the wider workforce. We also enable confident and informed use of AI tools in healthcare education by sharing our approach to AI adoption, our research into AI tools and continuing professional development (CPD) on techniques we have developed to integrate AI into content development.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Legal and ethical compliance</h3>

                <p>AI use must comply with current legislation, including intellectual property, copyright and ethical standards. Legal advice should be sought where appropriate. We do this by engaging relevant internal experts and following established processes, communicating openly with our clients, learners, content developers and SMEs about our use of AI, and producing statements and related documentation.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Cybersecurity</h3>

                <p>AI tools must meet NHS England’s cybersecurity standards, particularly when handling sensitive data or learner information. We do this by monitoring developments in this area, using officially procured AI tools and systems, and engaging with internal and external experts where appropriate.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Data protection and information governance</h3>

                <p>All AI use must align with NHS England’s information governance (IG) policies, including the UK General Data Protection Regulation (UK GDPR) and data protection impact assessment (DPIA) requirements. Data privacy and protection are non-negotiable. We support this by monitoring developments in this area, using officially procured AI tools and systems, and engaging with internal and external experts where appropriate.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Intellectual property protection</h3>

                <p>AI-generated content must respect licensing agreements and copyright frameworks. Attribution and permissions must be clearly documented. We support this through internal processes and contractual arrangements, clearly identifying the rights associated with the content used in our learning resources and how those resources may be shared.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Responsible procurement</h3>

                <p>AI tools should be procured with clear contractual protections, including clauses on data use, liability, copyright, bias monitoring and ongoing support. We do this by monitoring developments in this area, using officially procured AI tools and systems, and engaging with internal and external experts where appropriate.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Governance and oversight</h3>

                <p>AI adoption should be guided by established governance structures that include educational, technical, legal and information governance stakeholders. Transparency and inclusivity in decision-making are supported through clear documentation, approval at the appropriate level and sharing approaches to support the wider system.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Human oversight and bias mitigation</h3>

                <p>AI-generated outputs must be reviewed by qualified individuals to ensure accuracy, fairness and cultural sensitivity. Bias must be actively monitored and addressed. We do this by using our internal and external SMEs to quality-assure our content and investigating systems and processes that could make this process more consistent and, where appropriate, automated.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Transparency and accountability</h3>

                <p>AI use should be clearly documented and communicated. Where applicable, synthetic content should be labelled to maintain trust and clarity. We support this by producing and regularly reviewing this statement and related documents, labelling content that is wholly produced by AI, and sharing our approach with the wider community.</p>

                <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Support sustainable and purposeful use of AI</h3>

                <p>AI should be used only where it adds clear value to educational outcomes. We commit to minimising unnecessary computational load, selecting the most appropriate tools for each task and designing AI workflows that are environmentally responsible. This includes considering energy efficiency, reducing digital waste and aligning with NHS England’s sustainability goals.</p>

                <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l">Applications of generative AI</h2>

                <p>We actively explore emerging AI technologies through pilot projects and collaboration with partners, such as the NHS AI Exchange and the AI ambassadors programme, to ensure our learning resources remain future-ready.</p>

                <p>Examples of applications we have found effective in our workflow include:</p>

                <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
                    <li>Fact-checking source content against learning objectives identified by SMEs.</li>
                    <li>Comparing each iteration of a content plan against learning objectives to show where each objective has been addressed.</li>
                    <li>Generating formative questions with feedback.</li>
                    <li>Creating banks of assessment questions mapped to learning objectives.</li>
                    <li>Checking for and flagging contradictions in information.</li>
                    <li>Checking for and highlighting duplicated content.</li>
                    <li>Checking for the use of active language.</li>
                    <li>Simplifying dense technical information.</li>
                    <li>Creating learner personas using AI.</li>
                    <li>Summarising videos to create chapter summaries for use in learning content.</li>
                    <li>Generating long descriptions for explanatory illustrations.</li>
                    <li>Using AI agents to identify content themes and generate appropriate metadata for searching and categorising content on TEL digital learning platforms.</li>
                    <li>Generating storyboard images, such as an icon representing open-heart surgery, using a supplied style as inspiration.</li>
                    <li>Breaking down complex textual processes into visual representations.</li>
                    <li>Suggesting alternative ways to present information, such as creating a step-by-step process diagram from supplied information.</li>
                    <li>Setting the tone of voice and reading age.</li>
                    <li>Checking for inclusive and diverse representation.</li>
                    <li>Suggesting case studies, scenarios and narrative flows.</li>
                    <li>Identifying overly dense or complicated topics and suggesting ways to break them into manageable sections.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
        </article>

        @await Html.PartialAsync("~/Views/Shared/_PageReviewPartial.cshtml", new PageReviewModel { LastReviewedDate = new DateTime(2025, 04, 10), NextReviewDate = new DateTime(2028, 04, 10) })
    </div>

</div>
